Stop a forked child from resuming greenlets that were blocked at the fork.

The companion to cancelling the queued switches. That queue only holds greenlets
that were *runnable*; one that was blocked is parked on a watcher, and the loop
resumes it from there without consulting the queue. Both are copies in the
child, and neither may run: on an inherited TLS session such a copy writes a
record the parent will write again under the same sequence number, drawing
``bad_record_mac`` from the server, and on an inherited pipe it consumes bytes
the parent was capturing.

A watcher that is active at fork time is active precisely because something is
parked on it, and gevent's watchers are created once per object and started and
stopped around each wait, so stopping one in the child cancels the pending
resume without damaging the object. The child's next wait on that socket or file
starts it again, which is what lets a child of a plain ``os.fork()`` carry on
using what it inherited, as :mod:`gevent.os` documents and as gevent's own
bind/fork/accept server pattern requires.

Only waits are cancelled, and only other greenlets' waits. A watcher whose
callback is a ``Waiter.switch`` is one a greenlet is blocked on; anything else is
left alone, including signal handlers, which a child inherits by ordinary POSIX
rules, and :class:`gevent.Timeout`, whose timer may belong to a ``with`` block
the forking greenlet is still inside.

Finding those watchers costs one ``gc.get_objects()`` walk per fork in the
child, which measured about 2ms against a 690,000 object heap, against 70ms for
the spawn it accompanies.
